course: reflection
lesson: 30
title: Getting through it: who helps, what helps, and looking back
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Months after the worst year of her life, Zoe looks back: Grandad died, her best friend moved away, and she was bullied for a whole term; at the time she lay awake thinking it would never end, and now she thinks: "I didn't think I'd get through it. But I'm still here, and some days are even good."
explain: Over the year the heavy days got fewer; she still has bad days, but they don't last as long; what helped was Mum, her counsellor, her new friends and her diary; what didn't was the thought that it would last forever, because that "forever" turned out to be wrong.
explain: This whole course has told the same story many times, about grief, guilt, fights, being left out, failing and the tricks of the night mind: the first nights are the loudest, the feeling changes over days and weeks, the looping thought slowly loosens, and you come to understand things you could not see at the start.
explain: Across all those stories, the same things helped: saying the looping thought out loud to someone you trust, putting right what can be put right, small steps back into normal life, writing things down, and grown-ups who know how to help; hiding, pretending and waiting for it to vanish on its own helped least.
explain: Being "resilient" does not mean never being knocked down; it means getting back up, usually with help, one day at a time, and being the bit of help for someone else when it's their turn.
fact: Resilient means able to bounce back after hard times; it grows with help, talking and time, and does not mean never feeling bad.
fact: After a very hard time, bad days usually become fewer and shorter over months, though some still come.
fact: Parents would usually rather know about a problem and worry than not know; "bottling it up" means keeping feelings inside, which tends to make them heavier.
fact: "A problem shared is a problem halved" is a saying meaning that talking about a problem makes it feel smaller.
fact: Writing worries in a diary, sometimes called journaling or a "brain dump", can help sort them out and calm the mind at bedtime.
fact: Trusted adults include parents, teachers, coaches, school nurses, neighbours and relatives; many of them notice when a child is struggling.
fact: Going with a friend to get help can make it easier for them to go at all.
fact: Even strong grown-ups go through hard times and get help; hearing that helps children feel normal.
fact: A calm kit is a small set of things that help when feelings get big, such as a stress ball, a favourite photo and a breathing card; it often works better after practice.
fact: Anyone can need help; laughing at someone for seeing a counsellor is unkind, and the one laughing may need help one day too.
fact: A support network is the set of people you can turn to, such as family, teachers, the school nurse and helplines; knowing it in advance makes asking easier.
fact: Anger can be managed with practice, such as counting to ten and walking away; counsellors can teach these skills.
fact: Across most hard events, the things that help are talking to someone you trust, putting right what you can, small steps and time; hiding, pretending and waiting alone help least.
exercise: Zoe had a terrible year: a death, a friend moving away, and bullying. Looking back, what does she notice? | The heavy days got fewer, and she got through it | It was every bit as bad at the end of the year as it was at the start | Nothing at all helped, it just stopped one day | She has forgotten everything about that year
exercise: What does "resilient" mean? | Able to bounce back, often with help | Never feeling sad about anything at all | Being far too tough to ever need anybody | Hiding feelings so well that nobody knows
exercise: Which thought was wrong for Zoe? | "This will last forever" | "Some days are good" | "I'm still here" | "Mum helped me get through it"
exercise: Noah's mum says, "You can always talk to me," but he keeps it inside so she won't worry. What is true? | Parents would rather know and worry than not know | Mums never really want to hear about their children's problems | It's always kinder to keep problems inside | Talking makes problems twice as big
exercise: What does "a problem shared is a problem halved" mean? | Talking about a problem makes it feel smaller | Every problem must be split exactly in half between two people | Sharing problems makes them double | Only half of problems can be shared
exercise: Sophie writes her worries in a diary every night. Why does it help? | Writing sorts the worries out and calms her mind | The diary somehow solves the worries for her while she sleeps | It means she never has to talk to anyone | The worries move into her sister's head
exercise: Max's coach noticed he was quiet and asked how things were. What does this show? | Many grown-ups, like coaches, can be trusted adults | Only parents are allowed to help children | Coaches only care about winning matches, never about players' lives | Max should not have told the coach anything
exercise: Lily said to her sad friend, "Let's tell Miss together." Why might that work? | Going together makes it easier | Teachers only listen to pairs of children | Her friend can stay silent the whole time | It means Lily takes the blame for it
exercise: Jack's dad told him he once went through a hard time and got help. How did Jack feel over the months? | Closer to his dad | Ashamed of his dad | Worried about his dad | Bored by his dad's story
exercise: What lesson did Jack take? | Even strong people need help sometimes | Strong people never go through any hard times at all | Dads should never talk about feelings | Getting help is only for children
exercise: Isla has a calm kit. What's in it? | A stress ball, a favourite photo and a breathing card | A phone, sweets and a games console | Her homework, a pencil, a ruler and a rubber | A torch, a whistle and a map
exercise: At first, Isla's calm kit felt silly. What changed after a few weeks? | She calmed down more quickly when using it | She threw the whole kit away and felt much better without it | It stopped working completely | She needed a much bigger kit
exercise: Ben laughed at his friend for seeing a counsellor, then needed one himself. What did he learn? | Anyone can need help, so laughing at it is unkind | Counsellors only help people whose grandads die | He was right to laugh at his friend, and he just got unlucky later | It's fine to laugh as long as you never go
exercise: Ava's teacher gave the class a list of people to talk to. What is that list called? | A support network | A friendship chart | A class register of names | A family tree of helpers
exercise: Ethan used to break things when angry. What does he do now? | Counts to ten and walks away | Breaks only his own things now | Keeps the anger hidden inside | Shouts and shouts until it goes away
exercise: Across the whole course, which helped least after hard events? | Hiding, pretending and waiting alone | Talking to someone you trust | Putting right what can be put right | Small steps back into normal life
test: Zoe's mate goes, "you're like, lowkey a legend for getting through that." ("Lowkey" means kind of.) What's the truest version? | She got through it with help, and resilience grew with it | She did it all on her own and never needed anyone's help | She was never really upset at all | She's a legend because she never cried
test: Noah thinks, "I'll keep it inside so Mum won't worry." What would probably happen if he kept bottling it up? | The problem would feel heavier and he'd stay alone with it | The problem would solve itself quickly | His mum would never have to worry about anything ever again | He'd feel lighter every single week
test: Sophie's nan from Cornwall says, "That diary's a proper job, my 'ansum." ("Proper job" means really good; "my 'ansum" means my dear.) Why might the nan be right? | Writing worries down can calm the mind | Diaries keep monsters out of bedrooms | Writing makes the worries come true | Diaries are only really meant for grandmothers to keep
test: Max says, "I didn't think a coach would care." What's true about trusted adults? | Coaches, teachers and nurses can be trusted adults too | Only parents are ever trusted adults | Coaches are not allowed to talk about feelings | Children should only ever talk to friends
test: Lily's friend goes, "I can't go to Miss alone, I'll die of cringe." ("Cringe" means embarrassment.) What helps? | Going together, so she isn't alone | Telling her to sort it out herself | Telling Miss without her knowing | Waiting until the end of term
test: Jack's mate says, "your dad needed help? that's sus." ("Sus" means suspicious, dodgy.) What's the truth? | Even strong people need help sometimes | Needing help means you are weak | Dads should hide hard times forever | Getting help is something a dad should always keep hidden
test: In a cartoon, the hero gets over every bad thing by the end of the episode, alone. What does this course show instead? | Hard feelings usually ease over weeks, with help from others | Real people always recover from bad things within twenty minutes | Getting help means you aren't a real hero | Talking to anyone makes feelings worse
test: Isla says, "It actually helps." Her brother scoffs, "a stress ball? that's so cheugy." ("Cheugy" means uncool, try-hard.) Who is right? | Isla; tools ready for big feelings help with practice | Her brother; calm kits never help anyone | Her brother; you should just shout it out | Neither; feelings can't be calmed at all
test: Ben says, "I was such a jerk," about laughing at his mate's counselling. What's the best next step? | Say sorry to his friend and keep getting help | Pretend he never laughed at all | Laugh at someone else's problems instead so he feels better | Stop seeing the counsellor so nobody laughs
test: What would happen if Ava had never seen the list of who to talk to? | She might have thought there was nobody to ask | She would never need help with anything | She would already know everybody she could talk to anyway | The list would have come to her by magic
test: Ethan's cousin from Johannesburg says, "Now now you lose it, then just now you're calm, hey?" ("Now now" means very soon; "just now" means a bit later.) What does Ethan say he has learned? | He still gets angry, but he's the boss of it now | He never gets angry at all any more | Anger can only ever be stopped by breaking things until it's gone | Counsellors make people forget they were angry
test: A kid asks, "So what's the one thing that helps with ALL the stuff in this course?" Which is closest? | Telling someone you trust, and small steps | Hiding it until it goes away on its own | Pretending to be fine so nobody worries | Doing everything alone to be strong
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises resilience? | The capacity to recover from adversity, which commonly develops with assistance and time | The total absence of distress in the face of all adversity | An innate quality possessed only by a minority of persons | The concealment of all emotion from family and friends
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a child is advised against "bottling up" his difficulties. | Concealed difficulties tend to become heavier and remain unaddressed | Parents are prohibited from assisting with disclosed difficulties | Disclosure invariably doubles the magnitude of the difficulty | Difficulties disappear entirely when concealed for a period of one month or more
exam: It is given that a pupil possesses a list of persons to whom she may turn in difficulty. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Such a support network renders the seeking of help easier | Such a list is of no value to any pupil whatsoever | The list is to be consulted solely by teachers | Possession of the list obviates any need for help
exam: In respect of the measures most commonly found helpful following distressing events, determine which statement is correct. | Speaking with a trusted person, remedying what can be remedied, and gradual steps over time | Concealment, pretence, and solitary waiting for the distress to pass | The immediate abandonment of all ordinary activities and friendships indefinitely | Repeated private rehearsal of the distressing event without disclosure
